      <main id="content"><h1>FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle: mathematical reading route</h1><p>Read the statements and derivations in source order. Every result has its own optional Lean statement and proof. Source assumptions, library reuse and unproved boundaries are kept explicit.</p><ol><li><a href="#decl-autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgauss-dbde54b019">Actual proximal Gaussian oracle with sharp centered concentration</a></li></ol><section id="decl-autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgauss-dbde54b019"><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Actual proximal Gaussian oracle with sharp centered concentration</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle</code> · theorem · <a href="index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h3>Mathematical statement</h3><p class="reader-claim">Produce a measurable exact proximal selector p satisfying p+eta*gradientV(p)=y, the quantitative quadratic minimum and uniqueness, and equal-eta nonexpansivity. The literal G(s,z)=gradientV(p_s+sqrt(eta_s)*z) is jointly measurable and has equal-eta input/noise Lipschitz constants 1 and sqrteta. Produce the genuine Markov law K_s=G(s,-)_#stdGaussian with vector first Bochner moment. For its own mean m_s, every a and signed t have an integrable exponential and integral &lt;=exp(eta_s*t^2*norm(a)^2/2). This covers (3.2) and (4.3)-(4.5), without identifying K with the RGO posterior or HMC transition.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[F_s(x)=V(x)+\|x-y_s\|^2/(2\eta_s),\quad p_s+\eta_s\nabla V(p_s)=y_s,\quad G(s,z)=\nabla V(p_s+\sqrt{\eta_s}z),\quad K_s=G(s,\cdot)_\#\gamma,\quad m_s=\int w\,dK_s,\quad\int e^{t\langle a,w-m_s\rangle}dK_s\le e^{\eta_st^2\|a\|^2/2}.\]</div><h4>Proof roadmap</h4><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Derive the real gradient estimates</strong><p>Reuse the actual C2 Hessian-to-gradient and strong-convexity APIs. The source upper bound supplies gradientV Lipschitz1. The convex gradient-step theorem supplies id-gradientV Lipschitz1, and convexity supplies monotonicity. None is a theorem premise.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Construct the actual pointwise damped map</strong><p>For each actual positive eta, T_s contracts with its own coefficient eta_s/(1+eta_s)&lt;1. The coefficients may approach one over the parameter space. Banach fixed-point construction is pointwise and makes no uniform-parameter contraction or runtime assumption.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Obtain joint measurability from actual iterates</strong><p>Every iterate T_s^n(0) is measurable in s from actual measurable eta/y and the continuous id-minus-gradient map. Pointwise Banach convergence with the actual c_s and measurable-limit theorem give a measurable p. Uniform convergence and an upper eta cap are unnecessary; no measurable selector is supplied.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Recover the exact source equation</strong><p>Multiply the actual fixed-point equation by positive 1+eta and cancel the scalar inverse. The resulting equality is the literal proximal first-order condition for the source quadratic potential.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Prove the genuine minimum and uniqueness</strong><p>Differentiate the true quadratic regularization. Its gradient vanishes at the constructed p. Existing strong-convexity and first-order bounds give the exact coefficient (kappa^-1+eta^-1)/2. Positivity forces norm(z-p)^2=0 for any competing lower point.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Keep nonexpansivity at fixed eta</strong><p>Subtract the two literal proximal equations only when eta_s=eta_t. Monotonicity bounds the inner product below by the squared proximal distance. Cauchy-Schwarz and positive-norm cancellation give nonexpansivity; a zero-distance branch is handled separately.</p></li><li><span>7</span><strong>Use the literal Gaussian gradient evaluator</strong><p>The actual measurable p, eta square root and noise coordinate give a jointly measurable input to the continuous gradient. No posterior identification or oracle witness is used.</p></li><li><span>8</span><strong>Prove the exact input and noise constants</strong><p>Apply the true gradient Lipschitz bound, split the input difference with the triangle inequality and use proximal nonexpansivity at equal eta. The literal square-root coefficient stays outside the noise norm. This supplies both source inequalities (4.4)-(4.5).</p></li><li><span>9</span><strong>Realize the actual Markov kernel</strong><p>Take the product of the identity kernel and the constant standard Gaussian kernel, then map it by G. Joint measurability proves it Markov; dirac-product and map-map give the exact pointwise pushforward. This gradient-output law is distinct from the RGO posterior and HMC transitions.</p></li><li><span>10</span><strong>Produce the actual vector first moment</strong><p>For fixed s the already produced noise Lipschitz bound yields normG(s,z)&lt;=sqrt(eta_s) norm(z)+normG(s,0). Genuine Gaussian norm L1 and measurability imply vector Bochner integrability. Pushforward integrability proves the identity observable is integrable under the same actual output law K_s.</p></li><li><span>11</span><strong>Apply the canonical domain leaf to the actual direction</strong><p>For every actual vector a, Cauchy-Schwarz and the true noise bound give the scalar observable z-&gt;inner(a,G(s,z)) Lipschitz with constant norm(a)sqrt(eta_s). Apply the shared Fernique-domain theorem for every real t, including negative t and zero direction.</p></li><li><span>12</span><strong>Identify the actual center and pull back the law</strong><p>The Bochner pushforward identity gives m_s=int G(s,z)d gamma. Integral commutation with the continuous inner-product functional gives int f_s,a d gamma=inner(a,m_s). The integrability pushforward equivalence then yields every actual centered directional exponential under K_s. This is the output own mean; no equality with a smoothed gradient is assumed or proved.</p></li><li><span>13</span><strong>Apply sharp concentration at the actual own mean</strong><p>Push the genuine vector mean back through the same actual Gaussian gradient law, commute inner product with its Bochner integral and apply the canonical sharp concentration theorem to f_s,a(z)=inner(a,G(s,z)). Its internally established Lipschitz constant is norm(a)sqrteta. Positivity of eta gives (norm(a)sqrteta)^2=eta norm(a)^2; the coefficient is exactly one half for every signed t, with no step cap or smoothed-gradient mean substitution.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h4>All objects and hypotheses</h4><ul><li>Source beta=1, alpha=kappa^-1, real kappa&gt;=1; actual C2 V with kappa^-1*normv^2 &lt;= D2V(x)[v,v] &lt;= normv^2 for every x,v.</li><li>Finite real complete Hilbert/Borel full space E, including dimension zero; arbitrary measurable parameter space S; actual measurable eta:S-&gt;R and y:S-&gt;E with eta(s)&gt;0 for every s. These coordinate-free/parameter-space extensions are disclosed. Printed Lemma4.2 inherits eta&gt;0 from Section3.1; the eta&lt;=1 premise of neighboring Lemma4.1 is not consumed.</li><li>No supplied proximal point, minimizer, measurable selector, gradient estimate, Lipschitz bound, probability kernel, mean or concentration certificate. Exact witnesses, first moments, signed Laplace domains and sharp coefficient are produced internally. The numerical proximal call cap eta&lt;=1/(2 beta) remains a separate computational contract.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h3>Mathematical proof</h3><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>1. Derive the real gradient estimates</h5><p>Reuse the actual C2 Hessian-to-gradient and strong-convexity APIs. The source upper bound supplies gradientV Lipschitz1. The convex gradient-step theorem supplies id-gradientV Lipschitz1, and convexity supplies monotonicity. None is a theorem premise.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\operatorname{Lip}(\nabla V)\le1,\quad\operatorname{Lip}(I-\nabla V)\le1,\quad\langle\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(z),x-z\rangle\ge0.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>actual_gradient_bounds; QuadraticRegularization; SmoothnessEquivalences; GradientDescentContraction; StrongConvexFirstOrder</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>2. Construct the actual pointwise damped map</h5><p>For each actual positive eta, T_s contracts with its own coefficient eta_s/(1+eta_s)&lt;1. The coefficients may approach one over the parameter space. Banach fixed-point construction is pointwise and makes no uniform-parameter contraction or runtime assumption.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[T_s(x)=(1+\eta_s)^{-1}[y_s+\eta_s(x-\nabla V(x))],\quad c_s=\eta_s/(1+\eta_s)&lt;1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>parameterized_damped_point; ContractingWith</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>3. Obtain joint measurability from actual iterates</h5><p>Every iterate T_s^n(0) is measurable in s from actual measurable eta/y and the continuous id-minus-gradient map. Pointwise Banach convergence with the actual c_s and measurable-limit theorem give a measurable p. Uniform convergence and an upper eta cap are unnecessary; no measurable selector is supplied.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[p_s=\lim_{n\to\infty}T_s^n(0),\quad s\mapsto p_s\text{ measurable, even when }\sup_s c_s=1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ContractingWith.fixedPoint; tendsto_iterate_fixedPoint; measurable_of_tendsto_metrizable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>4. Recover the exact source equation</h5><p>Multiply the actual fixed-point equation by positive 1+eta and cancel the scalar inverse. The resulting equality is the literal proximal first-order condition for the source quadratic potential.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[p_s+\eta_s\nabla V(p_s)=y_s.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ContractingWith.fixedPoint_isFixedPt; smul_smul; mul_inv_cancel₀</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>5. Prove the genuine minimum and uniqueness</h5><p>Differentiate the true quadratic regularization. Its gradient vanishes at the constructed p. Existing strong-convexity and first-order bounds give the exact coefficient (kappa^-1+eta^-1)/2. Positivity forces norm(z-p)^2=0 for any competing lower point.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[F_s(p_s)+\tfrac12(\kappa^{-1}+\eta_s^{-1})\|z-p_s\|^2\le F_s(z),\quad F_s(z)\le F_s(p_s)\iff z=p_s.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>actual_quadratic_minimum; HasFDerivAt.norm_sq; QuadraticRegularization; StrongConvexFirstOrder.firstOrder_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>6. Keep nonexpansivity at fixed eta</h5><p>Subtract the two literal proximal equations only when eta_s=eta_t. Monotonicity bounds the inner product below by the squared proximal distance. Cauchy-Schwarz and positive-norm cancellation give nonexpansivity; a zero-distance branch is handled separately.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\eta_s=\eta_t\Longrightarrow\|p_s-p_t\|^2\le\langle y_s-y_t,p_s-p_t\rangle\Longrightarrow\|p_s-p_t\|\le\|y_s-y_t\|.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gradient_inner_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn; real_inner_le_norm; norm_nonneg</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>7. Use the literal Gaussian gradient evaluator</h5><p>The actual measurable p, eta square root and noise coordinate give a jointly measurable input to the continuous gradient. No posterior identification or oracle witness is used.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[G(s,z)=\nabla V(p_s+\sqrt{\eta_s}z),\quad G\text{ measurable on }S\times E.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Measurable.comp; Measurable.sqrt; Measurable.smul; LipschitzWith.continuous</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>8. Prove the exact input and noise constants</h5><p>Apply the true gradient Lipschitz bound, split the input difference with the triangle inequality and use proximal nonexpansivity at equal eta. The literal square-root coefficient stays outside the noise norm. This supplies both source inequalities (4.4)-(4.5).</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\eta_s=\eta_t\Longrightarrow\|G(s,z)-G(t,w)\|\le\|y_s-y_t\|+\sqrt{\eta_s}\|z-w\|.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>LipschitzWith.dist_le_mul; norm_add_le; norm_smul; Real.sqrt_nonneg</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>9. Realize the actual Markov kernel</h5><p>Take the product of the identity kernel and the constant standard Gaussian kernel, then map it by G. Joint measurability proves it Markov; dirac-product and map-map give the exact pointwise pushforward. This gradient-output law is distinct from the RGO posterior and HMC transitions.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[K=(\mathrm{id}\times\gamma).\mathrm{map}(G),\quad K_s=G(s,\cdot)_\#\gamma,\quad K_s(E)=1.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>Kernel.id; Kernel.const; Kernel.prod; Kernel.IsMarkovKernel.map; Measure.dirac_prod; Measure.map_map</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>10. Produce the actual vector first moment</h5><p>For fixed s the already produced noise Lipschitz bound yields normG(s,z)&lt;=sqrt(eta_s) norm(z)+normG(s,0). Genuine Gaussian norm L1 and measurability imply vector Bochner integrability. Pushforward integrability proves the identity observable is integrable under the same actual output law K_s.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\|G(s,z)\|\le\sqrt{\eta_s}\|z\|+\|G(s,0)\|,\quad G(s,\cdot)\in L^1(\gamma;E),\quad\mathrm{id}\in L^1(K_s;E).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>IsGaussian.integrable_id; Integrable.mono&#x27;; integrable_map_measure</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>11. Apply the canonical domain leaf to the actual direction</h5><p>For every actual vector a, Cauchy-Schwarz and the true noise bound give the scalar observable z-&gt;inner(a,G(s,z)) Lipschitz with constant norm(a)sqrt(eta_s). Apply the shared Fernique-domain theorem for every real t, including negative t and zero direction.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f_{s,a}(z)=\langle a,G(s,z)\rangle,\quad\operatorname{Lip}(f_{s,a})\le\|a\|\sqrt{\eta_s},\quad e^{t(f_{s,a}-\int f_{s,a}d\gamma)}\in L^1(\gamma).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz; abs_real_inner_le_norm; LipschitzWith.of_dist_le_mul</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>12. Identify the actual center and pull back the law</h5><p>The Bochner pushforward identity gives m_s=int G(s,z)d gamma. Integral commutation with the continuous inner-product functional gives int f_s,a d gamma=inner(a,m_s). The integrability pushforward equivalence then yields every actual centered directional exponential under K_s. This is the output own mean; no equality with a smoothed gradient is assumed or proved.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[m_s=\int w\,dK_s=\int G(s,z)\,d\gamma,\quad\int e^{t\langle a,w-m_s\rangle}\,dK_s(w)&lt;\infty\quad(a\in E,t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integral_map; integral_inner; inner_sub_right; integrable_map_measure</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>13. Apply sharp concentration at the actual own mean</h5><p>Push the genuine vector mean back through the same actual Gaussian gradient law, commute inner product with its Bochner integral and apply the canonical sharp concentration theorem to f_s,a(z)=inner(a,G(s,z)). Its internally established Lipschitz constant is norm(a)sqrteta. Positivity of eta gives (norm(a)sqrteta)^2=eta norm(a)^2; the coefficient is exactly one half for every signed t, with no step cap or smoothed-gradient mean substitution.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\int f_{s,a}\,d\gamma=\langle a,m_s\rangle,\quad\int e^{t\langle a,w-m_s\rangle}dK_s\le e^{(\|a\|\sqrt{\eta_s})^2t^2/2}=e^{\eta_st^2\|a\|^2/2}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integral_map; integral_inner; inner_sub_right; AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz; Real.sq_sqrt</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h3>Lean verification</h3><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle"><summary>Lean statement · full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle</summary><p>Produce a measurable exact proximal selector p satisfying p+eta*gradientV(p)=y, the quantitative quadratic minimum and uniqueness, and equal-eta nonexpansivity. The literal G(s,z)=gradientV(p_s+sqrt(eta_s)*z) is jointly measurable and has equal-eta input/noise Lipschitz constants 1 and sqrteta. Produce the genuine Markov law K_s=G(s,-)_#stdGaussian with vector first Bochner moment. For its own mean m_s, every a and signed t have an integrable exponential and integral &lt;=exp(eta_s*t^2*norm(a)^2/2). This covers (3.2) and (4.3)-(4.5), without identifying K with the RGO posterior or HMC transition.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle
    {E S : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    [MeasurableSpace S] {V : E → ℝ} {κ : ℝ}
    (hκ : 1 ≤ κ) (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, κ⁻¹*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ≤ ‖v‖^2)
    {eta : S → ℝ} {y : S → E} (heta : Measurable eta) (hy : Measurable y)
    (hpos : ∀ s, 0 &lt; eta s) :
    let F := fun s x =&gt; V x + (eta s)⁻¹/2*‖x-y s‖^2
    ∃ p : S → E, Measurable p ∧
      (∀ s, p s + eta s • gradient V (p s) = y s) ∧
      (∀ s z, F s (p s) + (κ⁻¹+(eta s)⁻¹)/2*‖z-p s‖^2 ≤ F s z ∧
        (F s z ≤ F s (p s) ↔ z=p s)) ∧
      (∀ s t, eta s = eta t → ‖p s-p t‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖) ∧
      let G := fun q : S × E =&gt; gradient V (p q.1+Real.sqrt (eta q.1) • q.2)
      Measurable G ∧
      (∀ s t z w, eta s = eta t →
        ‖G (s,z)-G (t,w)‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖) ∧
      ∃ K : ProbabilityTheory.Kernel S E, ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel K ∧
        (∀ s, K s = (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E).map (fun z =&gt; G (s,z))) ∧
        ∀ s, MeasureTheory.Integrable (fun w : E =&gt; w) (K s) ∧
          ∀ (a : E) (t : ℝ), MeasureTheory.Integrable
            (fun w =&gt; Real.exp (t * inner ℝ a (w - ∫ v, v ∂K s))) (K s) ∧
            (∫ w, Real.exp (t * inner ℝ a (w - ∫ v, v ∂K s)) ∂K s) ≤
              Real.exp (eta s * t^2 * ‖a‖^2 / 2)</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgaussianoracle.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle.full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle"><summary>Lean proof · full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle</summary><p>Derive the real gradient estimates; Construct the actual pointwise damped map; Obtain joint measurability from actual iterates; Recover the exact source equation; Prove the genuine minimum and uniqueness; Keep nonexpansivity at fixed eta; Use the literal Gaussian gradient evaluator; Prove the exact input and noise constants; Realize the actual Markov kernel; Produce the actual vector first moment; Apply the canonical domain leaf to the actual direction; Identify the actual center and pull back the law; Apply sharp concentration at the actual own mean</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem full_range_proximal_gaussian_oracle
    {E S : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    [MeasurableSpace S] {V : E → ℝ} {κ : ℝ}
    (hκ : 1 ≤ κ) (hV : ContDiff ℝ 2 V)
    (hH : ∀ x v : E, κ⁻¹*‖v‖^2 ≤ fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ∧
      fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x v v ≤ ‖v‖^2)
    {eta : S → ℝ} {y : S → E} (heta : Measurable eta) (hy : Measurable y)
    (hpos : ∀ s, 0 &lt; eta s) :
    let F := fun s x =&gt; V x + (eta s)⁻¹/2*‖x-y s‖^2
    ∃ p : S → E, Measurable p ∧
      (∀ s, p s + eta s • gradient V (p s) = y s) ∧
      (∀ s z, F s (p s) + (κ⁻¹+(eta s)⁻¹)/2*‖z-p s‖^2 ≤ F s z ∧
        (F s z ≤ F s (p s) ↔ z=p s)) ∧
      (∀ s t, eta s = eta t → ‖p s-p t‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖) ∧
      let G := fun q : S × E =&gt; gradient V (p q.1+Real.sqrt (eta q.1) • q.2)
      Measurable G ∧
      (∀ s t z w, eta s = eta t →
        ‖G (s,z)-G (t,w)‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖) ∧
      ∃ K : ProbabilityTheory.Kernel S E, ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel K ∧
        (∀ s, K s = (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E).map (fun z =&gt; G (s,z))) ∧
        ∀ s, MeasureTheory.Integrable (fun w : E =&gt; w) (K s) ∧
          ∀ (a : E) (t : ℝ), MeasureTheory.Integrable
            (fun w =&gt; Real.exp (t * inner ℝ a (w - ∫ v, v ∂K s))) (K s) ∧
            (∫ w, Real.exp (t * inner ℝ a (w - ∫ v, v ∂K s)) ∂K s) ≤
              Real.exp (eta s * t^2 * ‖a‖^2 / 2) := by
  let F := fun s x =&gt; V x+(eta s)⁻¹/2*‖x-y s‖^2
  obtain ⟨hLip,hMinus,_hsc,hmono⟩ := actual_gradient_bounds hκ hV hH
  obtain ⟨p,hp,heq⟩ := parameterized_damped_point hMinus heta hy hpos
  have hmin := actual_quadratic_minimum hκ hV hH hpos heq
  have hnexp : ∀ s t, eta s=eta t → ‖p s-p t‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖ := by
    intro s t ht
    have hsub : y s-y t=(p s-p t)+eta s • (gradient V (p s)-gradient V (p t)) := by
      rw [← heq s,← heq t,← ht]
      module
    have hpair : ‖p s-p t‖^2 ≤ inner ℝ (y s-y t) (p s-p t) := by
      calc
        _ ≤ ‖p s-p t‖^2+eta s*inner ℝ (gradient V (p s)-gradient V (p t)) (p s-p t) :=
          le_add_of_nonneg_right (mul_nonneg (hpos s).le (hmono (p s) (p t)))
        _ = _ := by
          rw [hsub,inner_add_left,real_inner_smul_left,real_inner_self_eq_norm_sq]
    have hb := hpair.trans (real_inner_le_norm (y s-y t) (p s-p t))
    by_cases hz : ‖p s-p t‖=0
    · simpa only [hz] using norm_nonneg (y s-y t)
    · have hpositive : 0 &lt; ‖p s-p t‖ := lt_of_le_of_ne (norm_nonneg _) (Ne.symm hz)
      nlinarith
  refine ⟨p,hp,heq,hmin,hnexp,?_⟩
  let G := fun q : S × E =&gt; gradient V (p q.1+Real.sqrt (eta q.1) • q.2)
  have hG : Measurable G := hLip.continuous.measurable.comp
    ((hp.comp measurable_fst).add ((heta.comp measurable_fst).sqrt.smul measurable_snd))
  have hGLip : ∀ s t z w, eta s=eta t →
      ‖G (s,z)-G (t,w)‖ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖ := by
    intro s t z w ht
    have h := hLip.dist_le_mul (p s+Real.sqrt (eta s) • z) (p t+Real.sqrt (eta t) • w)
    simp only [NNReal.coe_one,one_mul,dist_eq_norm] at h
    have hdiff : (p s+Real.sqrt (eta s) • z)-(p t+Real.sqrt (eta t) • w) =
        (p s-p t)+Real.sqrt (eta s) • (z-w) := by rw [← ht]; module
    calc
      _ ≤ ‖(p s+Real.sqrt (eta s) • z)-(p t+Real.sqrt (eta t) • w)‖ := h
      _ = ‖(p s-p t)+Real.sqrt (eta s) • (z-w)‖ := by rw [hdiff]
      _ ≤ ‖p s-p t‖+‖Real.sqrt (eta s) • (z-w)‖ := norm_add_le _ _
      _ ≤ ‖y s-y t‖+Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖ := by
        rw [norm_smul,Real.norm_eq_abs,abs_of_nonneg (Real.sqrt_nonneg _)]
        exact add_le_add (hnexp s t ht) le_rfl
  let K := (Kernel.id ×ₖ Kernel.const S (stdGaussian E)).map G
  have hK : IsMarkovKernel K := Kernel.IsMarkovKernel.map _ hG
  have hKs (s : S) : K s=(stdGaussian E).map (fun z =&gt; G (s,z)) := by
    dsimp only [K]
    rw [Kernel.map_apply _ hG,Kernel.prod_apply,Kernel.id_apply,Kernel.const_apply,
      Measure.dirac_prod,Measure.map_map hG (by fun_prop)]
    rfl
  have hGm (s : S) : Measurable (fun z =&gt; G (s,z)) :=
    hG.comp (measurable_const.prodMk measurable_id)
  have hGi (s : S) : Integrable (fun z =&gt; G (s,z)) (stdGaussian E) := by
    apply (((IsGaussian.integrable_id (μ := stdGaussian E)).norm.const_mul
      (Real.sqrt (eta s))).add (integrable_const ‖G (s,0)‖)).mono&#x27;
        (hGm s).aestronglyMeasurable
    filter_upwards with z
    have hd := hGLip s s z 0 rfl
    simp only [sub_self,norm_zero,zero_add,sub_zero] at hd
    calc
      ‖G (s,z)‖ = ‖(G (s,z)-G (s,0))+G (s,0)‖ := by rw [sub_add_cancel]
      _ ≤ ‖G (s,z)-G (s,0)‖+‖G (s,0)‖ := norm_add_le _ _
      _ ≤ Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z‖+‖G (s,0)‖ := add_le_add hd le_rfl
      _ = _ := rfl
  refine ⟨hG,hGLip,K,hK,hKs,fun s =&gt; ⟨?_,fun a t =&gt; ?_⟩⟩
  · rw [hKs s]
    exact (integrable_map_measure (by fun_prop) (hGm s).aemeasurable).mpr (hGi s)
  · have hf : LipschitzWith (Real.toNNReal (‖a‖*Real.sqrt (eta s)))
        (fun z =&gt; inner ℝ a (G (s,z))) := by
      apply LipschitzWith.of_dist_le_mul
      intro z w
      rw [dist_eq_norm,Real.norm_eq_abs,← inner_sub_right,
        Real.coe_toNNReal _ (mul_nonneg (norm_nonneg _) (Real.sqrt_nonneg _)),dist_eq_norm]
      have hd := hGLip s s z w rfl
      simp only [sub_self,norm_zero,zero_add] at hd
      calc
        |inner ℝ a (G (s,z)-G (s,w))| ≤ ‖a‖*‖G (s,z)-G (s,w)‖ :=
          abs_real_inner_le_norm _ _
        _ ≤ ‖a‖*(Real.sqrt (eta s)*‖z-w‖) :=
          mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left hd (norm_nonneg _)
        _ = (‖a‖*Real.sqrt (eta s))*‖z-w‖ := by ring
    have he :=
      (AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz
        (μ := stdGaussian E) hf).2 t
    refine ⟨?_,?_⟩
    · rw [hKs s,integral_map (hGm s).aemeasurable (by fun_prop)]
      apply (integrable_map_measure (by fun_prop) (hGm s).aemeasurable).mpr
      convert he using 1
      funext z
      rw [Function.comp_apply,inner_sub_right,integral_inner (hGi s) a]
    · have hm : (∫ v, v ∂K s) = ∫ z, G (s,z) ∂stdGaussian E := by
        rw [hKs s]
        exact integral_map (hGm s).aemeasurable (by fun_prop)
      rw [hm,hKs s,integral_map (hGm s).aemeasurable (by fun_prop)]
      calc
        _ = ∫ z, Real.exp (t * (inner ℝ a (G (s,z)) -
            ∫ v, inner ℝ a (G (s,v)) ∂stdGaussian E)) ∂stdGaussian E := by
          apply integral_congr_ae
          filter_upwards with z
          rw [inner_sub_right,integral_inner (hGi s) a]
        _ ≤ Real.exp (((Real.toNNReal (‖a‖*Real.sqrt (eta s)) : ℝ≥0) : ℝ)^2*t^2/2) :=
          AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz hf t
        _ = _ := by
          congr 1
          rw [Real.coe_toNNReal _ (mul_nonneg (norm_nonneg _) (Real.sqrt_nonneg _)),
            mul_pow,Real.sq_sqrt (hpos s).le]
          ring


end AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.FullRangeProximalGaussianOracle
end</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-examplecases-smoothedpicardhmc-fullrangeproximalgaussianoracle.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Locally compiled sharp centered Gaussian-output estimate (4.3), retained exact positive-step proximal/evaluator and (4.4)-(4.5), genuine first Bochner moments and all signed exponential domains. Independent mathematics/blind/source/publication/exact-commit/integration admission pending. Bias (4.2), first W2 inequality in (4.6), LSI/T2 adapter, full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS invariance/nonexplosion/hypocoercivity/implementation/work, both main results and actual-input precision/expected-query-cost composition remain open. Centers are actual Gaussian output means. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h3>Source and reuse</h3><h4>Samplinglib parents called</h4><ul><li><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-quadraticregularization-stro-17eea540ae"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.QuadraticRegularization.strongConvexOn_and_lipschitzWith_gradient_add_quadratic</code></a></li><li><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-hessianstrongconvexity-stron-258a330876"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Analysis.HessianStrongConvexity.strongConvexOn_univ_of_fderiv2_lower</code></a></li><li><a href="../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-analysis-smoothnessequivalences-upper-model-iff-fderiv2-upper.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Ana